+---
+name: code-review
+description: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes 鈥?Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/PRD asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to "review since X".
+---
+
+Two-axis review of the diff between `HEAD` and a fixed point the user supplies:
+
+- **Standards** 鈥?does the code conform to this repo's documented coding standards?
+- **Spec** 鈥?does the code faithfully implement the originating issue / PRD / spec?
+
+Both axes run as **parallel sub-agents** so they don't pollute each other's context, then this skill aggregates their findings.
+
+The issue tracker should have been provided to you 鈥?run `/setup-matt-pocock-skills` if `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` is missing.
+
+## Process
+
+### 1. Pin the fixed point
+
+Whatever the user said is the fixed point 鈥?a commit SHA, branch name, tag, `main`, `HEAD~5`, etc. If they didn't specify one, ask for it.
+
+Capture the diff command once: `git diff <fixed-point>...HEAD` (three-dot, so the comparison is against the merge-base). Also note the list of commits via `git log <fixed-point>..HEAD --oneline`.
+
+Before going further, confirm the fixed point resolves (`git rev-parse <fixed-point>`) and the diff is non-empty. A bad ref or empty diff should fail here 鈥?not inside two parallel sub-agents.
+
+### 2. Identify the spec source
+
+Look for the originating spec, in this order:
+
+1. Issue references in the commit messages (`#123`, `Closes #45`, GitLab `!67`, etc.) 鈥?fetch via the workflow in `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.
+2. A path the user passed as an argument.
+3. A PRD/spec file under `docs/`, `specs/`, or `.scratch/` matching the branch name or feature.
+4. If nothing is found, ask the user where the spec is. If they say there isn't one, the **Spec** sub-agent will skip and report "no spec available".
+
+### 3. Identify the standards sources
+
+Anything in the repo that documents how code should be written, such as `CODING_STANDARDS.md` or `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
+
+On top of whatever the repo documents, the Standards axis always carries the **smell baseline** below 鈥?a fixed set of Fowler code smells (_Refactoring_, ch.3) that applies even when a repo documents nothing. Two rules bind it:
+
+- **The repo overrides.** A documented repo standard always wins; where it endorses something the baseline would flag, suppress the smell.
+- **Always a judgement call.** Each smell is a labelled heuristic ("possible Feature Envy"), never a hard violation 鈥?and, like any standard here, skip anything tooling already enforces.
+
+Each smell reads *what it is* 鈫?*how to fix*; match it against the diff:
+
+- **Mysterious Name** 鈥?a function, variable, or type whose name doesn't reveal what it does or holds. 鈫?rename it; if no honest name comes, the design's murky.
+- **Duplicated Code** 鈥?the same logic shape appears in more than one hunk or file in the change. 鈫?extract the shared shape, call it from both.
+- **Feature Envy** 鈥?a method that reaches into another object's data more than its own. 鈫?move the method onto the data it envies.
+- **Data Clumps** 鈥?the same few fields or params keep travelling together (a type wanting to be born). 鈫?bundle them into one type, pass that.
+- **Primitive Obsession** 鈥?a primitive or string standing in for a domain concept that deserves its own type. 鈫?give the concept its own small type.
+- **Repeated Switches** 鈥?the same `switch`/`if`-cascade on the same type recurs across the change. 鈫?replace with polymorphism, or one map both sites share.
+- **Shotgun Surgery** 鈥?one logical change forces scattered edits across many files in the diff. 鈫?gather what changes together into one module.
+- **Divergent Change** 鈥?one file or module is edited for several unrelated reasons. 鈫?split so each module changes for one reason.
+- **Speculative Generality** 鈥?abstraction, parameters, or hooks added for needs the spec doesn't have. 鈫?delete it; inline back until a real need shows.
+- **Message Chains** 鈥?long `a.b().c().d()` navigation the caller shouldn't depend on. 鈫?hide the walk behind one method on the first object.
+- **Middle Man** 鈥?a class or function that mostly just delegates onward. 鈫?cut it, call the real target direct.
+- **Refused Bequest** 鈥?a subclass or implementer that ignores or overrides most of what it inherits. 鈫?drop the inheritance, use composition.
+
+### 4. Spawn both sub-agents in parallel
+
+Send a single message with two `Agent` tool calls. Use the `general-purpose` subagent for both.
+
+**Standards sub-agent prompt** 鈥?include:
+
+- The full diff command and commit list.
+- The list of standards-source files you found in step 3, **plus the smell baseline from step 3** pasted in full 鈥?the sub-agent has no other access to it.
+- The brief: "Report 鈥?per file/hunk where relevant 鈥?(a) every place the diff violates a documented standard: cite the standard (file + the rule); and (b) any baseline smell you spot: name it and quote the hunk. Distinguish hard violations from judgement calls 鈥?documented-standard breaches can be hard, but baseline smells are always judgement calls, and a documented repo standard overrides the baseline. Skip anything tooling enforces. Under 400 words."
+
+**Spec sub-agent prompt** 鈥?include:
+
+- The diff command and commit list.
+- The path or fetched contents of the spec.
+- The brief: "Report: (a) requirements the spec asked for that are missing or partial; (b) behaviour in the diff that wasn't asked for (scope creep); (c) requirements that look implemented but where the implementation looks wrong. Quote the spec line for each finding. Under 400 words."
+
+If the spec is missing, skip the Spec sub-agent and note this in the final report.
+
+### 5. Aggregate
+
+Present the two reports under `## Standards` and `## Spec` headings, verbatim or lightly cleaned. Do **not** merge or rerank findings 鈥?the two axes are deliberately separate (see _Why two axes_).
+
+End with a one-line summary: total findings per axis, and the worst issue _within each axis_ (if any). Don't pick a single winner across axes 鈥?that's the reranking the separation exists to prevent.
+
+## Why two axes
+
+A change can pass one axis and fail the other:
+
+- Code that follows every standard but implements the wrong thing 鈫?**Standards pass, Spec fail.**
+- Code that does exactly what the issue asked but breaks the project's conventions 鈫?**Spec pass, Standards fail.**
+
+Reporting them separately stops one axis from masking the other.
